Weak-LD controls (LD/genic < 0.07: 09247, 08172, 13132, 06028, 06126) have kernel LD 15–77% weaker
than either, but V_G/genic within 0.01–0.03; LD there is the small difference of two noisy
estimates and was not pursued.
=> Given the kernel's genic variance, Bulmer's recursion with drift is right once the LD is
resolved by position on the chromosome; the mean field over loci (the multilocus model) makes it
too strong, by 5–20% in LD, most at u = 5. Mechanism: under QTSim's crossover (only the lowest of
the points switches) the tightly linked pairs lie toward the chromosome's end; their loci's
covariance with the genotype H_i is reduced by their own negative LD (β of the last locus 0.1–0.6
against 0.5–1.0 for the first), and the generation of LD on a pair is H_i H_j/s, so the LD is
generated most weakly exactly where it decays most slowly. The mean field gives every pair the
average H². The same holds (scratch, not tabulated) on the C and Cctl inputs.
Site turnover (a) and drift are not needed to explain the static LD; the multilocus model's genic
variance is also too high (step 3), which further lowers its V_G/genic.

== step 3: first implementation (mean field, src/spectrum.jl ld = :dynamic) ==

Implemented as above, mean field over loci and effects (one β), turnover on, Barton's term kept as
in the default (it is 0 at λ = 0). Default :spectrum bit for bit unchanged (checked: emulate,
spectrum_equilibrium and a run_moments trajectory on two inputs, `isequal` before/after).
`validate/spectrum_ld_dev.jl static D+Dctl`, model/kernel at the move (equilibrium of each model):
  spectrum     V_G 1.281 (1.005–2.203)  genic 0.941 (0.483–1.022)  V_G/genic 1.549 (1.006–2.837)
  ld           V_G 0.967 (0.828–1.247)  genic 1.037 (0.988–1.323)  V_G/genic 0.914 (0.770–1.006)
  ld-noturn    V_G 0.957 (0.825–1.271)  genic 1.039 (0.988–1.504)  V_G/genic 0.879 (0.750–1.005)
  multilocus   V_G 0.979 (0.831–1.969)  genic 1.097 (0.993–2.766)  V_G/genic 0.856 (0.712–1.003)
The β² shielding raises the spectrum's genic variance from 0.94 to 1.04 of the kernel's; the LD is
still too strong (V_G/genic 0.91 of the kernel's, worst at u = 5: 05562 0.78, 07594 0.77, 14110
0.83), as step 2 predicts for the mean field. Next: resolve the LD by position (bins of loci),
derived from step 2, with the bins a numerical resolution checked for convergence, not a fit.

== step 4: LD resolved by position (ldbins) ==

Derived from step 2: the loci are cut into B bins of consecutive positions; the LD classes are
(bin a, bin b, ρ) with the exact split probabilities of every pair (pair_splits, 2% ρ bins as
pair_classes; QTLSim.ld_classes); each bin has its own β_b = V_b/g_b (V_b its genic share plus the
LD of its pairs, half of a pair shared with another bin); a pair class gains −ω_c β_a β_b g²/(2S).
The sites of all bins share ONE spectrum (the frequency distribution is not resolved by position),
so the per-site factors are the genic-weighted means over bins: directional Σ m_b β_b = V_G/genic
(the Gaussian response V_G λ/S is kept), stabilising Σ m_b β_b². With B = 1 it is step 3's model.
B is a numerical resolution: V_G/genic at equilibrium for B = 1/5/10/20/40/80 on five D inputs
(scratch bins.jl): 06159 0.881/0.8952/0.8955/0.8956/0.8956/0.8956; 01192 0.541/0.590/0.591/0.5915/
0.5916/0.5917; 14110 0.496/0.587/0.590/0.590/0.590/0.590; 12000 0.321/0.363/0.364/0.3646/0.3647/
0.3648; 05562 0.389/0.482/0.484/0.4846/0.4848/0.4848. Converged to < 3e-4 at B = 20: default
ldbins = 20 (cost: ~10k classes at 5000 loci, ≈ +25% on an equilibrium).
